###################################################################################################
#
# The pipeline is:
#
#   1. read the model with trimesh (GLB/glTF, OBJ, PLY, STL, ...), baking node
#      transforms and collecting one "part" per material;
#   2. hand the geometry to MeshLab (pymeshlab) through a binary PLY that carries
#      per-corner ("wedge") texture coordinates and a per-face texture index --
#      MeshLab's own PLY convention -- so that multi-texture models stay intact;
#   3. in MeshLab: weld duplicate vertices (scanner exports typically store every
#      triangle with its own three vertices, which also makes decimation
#      impossible), clean up, and simplify with quadric edge collapse -- the same
#      algorithm as Blender's Decimate modifier in Collapse mode, in MeshLab's
#      texture-aware variant that keeps UV seams consistent;
#   4. shrink and re-encode the textures as JPEG;
#   5. write a compact binary GLB (or OBJ/PLY/STL) with our own writers, so
#      that we control exactly what ends up in the file.
#
###################################################################################################

